<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest Hemingway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ernest Miller Hemingway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest Miller Hemingway was an American novelist, short-story writer and journalist whose economical, understated style influenced later 20th-century writers. Raised in Oak Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, he drove an ambulance for the Red Cross on the Italian Front in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and was seriously wounded in 1918. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1921 he wrote for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> Star, and he published The Sun Also Rises (1926), A Farewell to Arms (1929) and For Whom the Bell Tolls (1940), drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. The Old Man and the Sea (1952) won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction, and he received the 1954 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature. He died by suicide in Ketchum, Idaho, in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-hemingway.jpg" alt="Ernest Hemingway" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lloyd Arnold · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ErnestHemingway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernest Hemingway was born on 21 July 1899 in Oak Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, a conservative, affluent suburb west of Chicago, the second of six children of Clarence Edmonds Hemingway, a physician, and Grace Hall Hemingway, a musician. His mother taught him the cello. His father taught him woodcraft during summers on Walloon Lake in Michigan, where he learned to hunt, fish and camp.</p><p>Poor eyesight kept him out of the United States Army, and in December 1917 he volunteered for the American Red Cross Motor Corps as an ambulance driver in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. On 8 July 1918 his group came under mortar fire. Seriously wounded, he helped Italian soldiers to safety and was decorated with the Italian Silver Medal of Military Valor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1921 he met writers including Gertrude Stein, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a> and Ezra Pound. Stein, who referred to the expatriates of Montparnasse as the Lost Generation, became his mentor, though their relationship later deteriorated. The Sun Also Rises, written in spare, tight prose, appeared in 1926, and A Farewell to Arms was published on 27 September 1929.</p><p>In 1937 he signed with the North American Newspaper Alliance to cover the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as a front-line correspondent for Collier's from May 1944 to March 1945. In January 1954, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, two plane crashes left him seriously injured. In October 1954 he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature; still recuperating, he sent a speech to be read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Through the 1950s Hemingway reworked the material later published as A Moveable Feast. He was in poor health, and in October 1960 he was taken to Ketchum, Idaho. He died by suicide at his house there on 2 July 1961.</p><p>Hemingway's legacy to American literature is his style, which later writers either emulated or avoided. His books were burned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1933 as a monument of modern decadence. J. D. Salinger, who corresponded with him during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, acknowledged him as an influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest Hemingway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
